  "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2x+6\\cdot 0+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1+12 & =0+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+12 & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-1)^{2}=-11.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/ea1410ea792ac5fb09316bc6d417c2ba46f84c050491b1e351f360eac50deb6c.png)",
